WEBVTT

00:00.350 --> 00:07.458
An aircraft returns to service because authorised people can show which
requirement, instruction and revision governs the work.

00:07.458 --> 00:14.960
The physical task may happen in a hangar, but the approval depends on a
controlled chain of documentary evidence.

00:14.960 --> 00:20.883
When that chain is fragmented across engineering instructions, maintenance
publications, service information and referenced standards,

00:20.883 --> 00:24.832
finding the right source becomes part of the engineering workload.

00:24.832 --> 00:31.150
Cortessa is designed to help reviewers investigate that evidence without
taking the decision away from them.

00:32.150 --> 00:35.325
A controlled change rarely lives in one document.

00:35.325 --> 00:42.867
A requirement can lead to an engineering order, an instruction, an illustrated
publication, a service communication and an external

00:42.867 --> 00:43.264
standard.

00:43.264 --> 00:47.233
Each may carry its own applicability, revision and approval state.

00:47.233 --> 00:50.805
The review problem is not simply finding matching words.

00:50.805 --> 00:58.744
It is establishing whether the source is current, whether the reference is
present, whether the terminology still aligns and whether

00:58.744 --> 01:03.110
the evidence applies to the aircraft, configuration and task under review.

01:04.110 --> 01:11.728
Cortessa's target civil-aerospace deployment gives each authorised engineer a
dedicated, self-sufficient box inside the site boundary.

01:11.728 --> 01:17.918
Local documents, ingestion, index, on-device model and source viewer remain on
that box.

01:17.918 --> 01:22.679
Audit and Ask run locally against the engineer's approved corpus.

01:22.679 --> 01:26.488
Author remains in development and requires further testing.

01:26.488 --> 01:33.630
The engineer controls the working context and remains responsible for
applicability, interpretation and every revision.

01:34.630 --> 01:43.289
Engineer boxes are designed to connect through departmental nodes for
engineering, configuration, quality and airworthiness, then to an

01:43.289 --> 01:44.252
on-site hub.

01:44.252 --> 01:52.430
The complete exchange stays inside the air-gapped site, with
department-default scope and selected sharing only where authorised.

01:52.430 --> 01:59.646
This is the target site architecture, not a claim of a deployed production
network today.

01:59.646 --> 02:06.862
The offline revision contract and application foundations exist; authenticated
transport, key operations, scheduling, backup, recovery,

02:06.862 --> 02:10.230
site assurance and rollout remain deployment work.

02:11.230 --> 02:14.865
Audit is the centre of the Cortessa workflow.

02:14.865 --> 02:22.134
It works across the selected engineering pack rather than waiting for one
question at a time.

02:22.134 --> 02:28.495
It can surface supersession questions, orphaned references, terminology
differences, effectivity uncertainty and stale wording.

02:28.495 --> 02:35.310
Each candidate enters engineering review; it is not automatically an
airworthiness, regulatory or safety finding.

02:36.310 --> 02:40.969
Every audit result leads back to the passages that created it.

02:40.969 --> 02:47.322
The engineer can open the source, inspect surrounding wording and check
revision, effectivity and applicability.

02:47.322 --> 02:52.404
If evidence is absent, Cortessa can refuse rather than fill the gap.

02:52.404 --> 03:00.451
The engineer decides whether the issue is real, irrelevant, already controlled
or worth investigation, then records the outcome through

03:00.451 --> 03:01.722
the existing process.

03:01.722 --> 03:05.110
Verified shows provenance; it does not establish applicability.

03:06.110 --> 03:09.846
Configuration, effectivity and airworthiness significance require engineering
judgement.

03:09.846 --> 03:17.319
The responsible engineer decides which source governs, whether an apparent
conflict matters and what action follows.

03:17.319 --> 03:21.990
Cortessa makes the source route, uncertainty and review question visible.

03:21.990 --> 03:29.930
It does not approve technical data, release an aircraft, certify a repair or
sign an engineering decision.

03:29.930 --> 03:37.870
Existing design, quality and airworthiness authorities remain distinct, and
their formal records remain the record of approval.

03:38.870 --> 03:46.744
An engineer-approved revision can carry its signed identity, content hash,
parent lineage and approved audit context through the

03:46.744 --> 03:50.682
relevant departmental node and, where authorised, the on-site hub.

03:50.682 --> 03:57.244
Conflicting branches and missing ancestry stay explicit; the hub does not
choose an engineering answer.

03:57.244 --> 04:03.806
Receipt does not certify the document, supersede the local corpus or make it
searchable automatically.

04:03.806 --> 04:06.868
The receiving engineer governs import and use.

04:06.868 --> 04:13.430
The local Ledger supports inspection of activity but never replaces
configuration, airworthiness or approval records.

04:14.430 --> 04:17.861
Compare matched tasks against the current review baseline.

04:17.861 --> 04:26.439
Measure time to locate and open the governing source, reviewer effort, the
number and category of cited findings, reviewer agreement,

04:26.439 --> 04:30.299
appropriate refusals and the reproducibility of sampled source paths.

04:30.299 --> 04:34.159
Include difficult documents, superseded references and deliberately absent
evidence.

04:34.159 --> 04:42.308
Begin with one cleared pack and department, then test the controls required to
extend it through the site architecture.

04:42.308 --> 04:45.310
Record preparation and setup effort before expansion.

04:46.310 --> 04:48.682
Bring one controlled engineering pack.

04:48.682 --> 04:57.698
Agree the reviewers, baseline and evidence standard, then test the route from
local evidence work to governed departmental revision

04:57.698 --> 04:58.172
exchange.

04:58.172 --> 05:04.815
Cortessa claims no aircraft deployment, completed site rollout, certification,
dispatch improvement or safety outcome.

05:04.815 --> 05:10.034
The architecture must be demonstrated and assured in a representative
environment.

05:10.034 --> 05:13.830
Start a conversation about a scoped civil-aerospace evaluation.
